.TH SRS-GUI 1 "October 2026" "srs-sysinfo 2.1.0" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
srs-gui \- SRS system information viewer (GTK 3)
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B srs-gui
[\fB\-\-gui\-open=\fIPANEL\fR[,\fIPANEL\fR...]] [\fB\-\-gui\-tray\fR] [\fB\-\-gui\-classic\fR | \fB\-\-gui\-new\fR]
.br
.B srs-gui
\fB\-\-power\-recorder\fR
.br
.B srs-gui
\fB\-\-help\fR | \fB\-\-version\fR
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B srs-gui
is the graphical front end of SRS, a Linux system information viewer inspired by
SIV64X (System Information Viewer by Ray Hinchliffe, RH Software) and built to improve on it.
SRS is an independent project, not affiliated with SIV or its author. It shows CPU,
memory, sensor, storage, bus, GPU, network and operating-system information,
and includes a CPU burn-in / stress tester and benchmarks (Dhrystone / Whetstone, Cache & Memory,
Storage, SRS Pi, Crypto, Prime Sieve, IPI delay, memory latency).
SRS is not affiliated with or endorsed by RH Software.
.PP
The main window (new in 2.0) has a sidebar of pages: Overview, Processor, Memory,
Motherboard & BIOS, Graphics & audio, Storage, Network, Sensors, Power & cost, USB & PCI
devices, Operating system, Benchmarks & stress and Tools; \fBAlt+1\fR ... \fBAlt+9\fR,
\fBAlt+0\fR, \fBAlt+\-\fR, \fBAlt+=\fR and \fBAlt+Backspace\fR go to them in that order. The Overview answers "Is my PC OK?" with a
status light per check (temperatures against each sensor's own limit, drive health, hardware
errors, free disk space, memory in use, sensor alarms) and cards with the key facts. Each other
page shows key facts and the items as tabs; "All N items" is a searchable list of every item of
the page. The search box (\fBCtrl+F\fR) finds any item by name or id.
.PP
Since 2.1 the tabs can be arranged: add (\fB+\fR, \fBCtrl+T\fR), close (\fBCtrl+W\fR), replace,
move and keep them; each page's tabs and their order are remembered. Options set, per page, the
standard tabs, chosen ones or every item, and the tab bar (one scrolling row, wrapped rows or a
side list). Lineups save a whole arrangement: Basic, Enthusiast (the default), Expert and your
own, with export and import. Windows-only items and hardware the PC does not have are hidden
unless "Show hidden items" is on; items that come out empty are hidden too after Options \(->
"Check now" or the opt-in "Check daily" (off by default; it never runs the network items or Ping). Click a table's column heading to sort it (numbers by value with their
units, dates by date); Reset view, Copy and Save Report keep the item's own order. CPU Clocks
shows each clock at stock next to what is in effect (measured base clock, FCLK / UCLK / MCLK from
the SMU). The Startup programs tab opens on its read-only Report; Manage, next to it, turns
autostart entries and services off and on without deleting anything (Undo, Restore default, Add
to startup). A badge in the title bar says "Full access"
or "Limited (user)".
.PP
\fBPower & cost\fR (new in 2.1) estimates how much power the whole PC draws at the wall, with a
likely range: the processor (zenergy, amd_energy or a readable RAPL counter) and the graphics card
(amdgpu, Intel, nouveau) are measured, everything else is estimated from an editable parts list and
a power-supply efficiency curve. It shows the cost at your price (flat, tiered or time-of-use, any
currency, or an offline area average), records one row per minute when asked (off, for a set time,
or always), draws daily/weekly/monthly history, exports CSV and raises alarms as desktop
notifications. \fBThese are estimates, not a meter reading or a bill.\fR
.PP
The classic view is the SIV-style interface of SRS 1.1: a main window with SIV's button row and
every item in its own panel. Open it with Menu \(-> Classic View, or start in it with
\fB\-\-gui\-classic\fR or the option "Start in classic view".
.PP
Root-only data (SMBIOS serials, DIMM SPD, SMART, ACPI tables, MSRs) is read
from a cache filled by
.BR srs-root (1).
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BI \-\-gui\-open= PANEL
Open the named items or tools at start: in the main window an item opens on its page, in the
classic view as its own panel. For example
.BR sensors ", " processors ", " burnin ", " stress ", " bench ", " ipi ", " latency ", " pi ", " crypto ", " prime ", " cachemem ", " storagebench ", " idlookup ", " find .
.TP
.B \-\-gui\-tray
start in the system tray.
.TP
.B \-\-gui\-classic
start in the classic view: the SIV-style main window with its button row, every item in its own panel.
.TP
.B \-\-gui\-new
start in the main window with the sidebar (Overview and one page per category), even when
"Start in classic view" is set in the options.
.TP
.B \-\-gui\-open=power\-cost
open the Power & cost page.
.TP
.B \-\-power\-recorder
record Power & cost history with no window, following the recording setting on the page (used by
the optional systemd user service ~/.config/systemd/user/srs-power-recorder.service, which the
page's Setup tab writes and removes). Only one process records at a time.
.TP
.BR \-\-help ", " \-\-version
Print the command-line help or the version and exit (these are shared with
.BR sysreport-search (1)).
.SH USER GUIDE
Every tab and panel has a \fBGuide\fR button (also \fBF1\fR, and "Open guide for this item"
in its right-click menu) that opens the user guide at that item's section: in
Okular, Evince or Papers at the right page, otherwise the HTML guide in the web
browser. \fBMenu \(-> User Guide\fR opens the start; \fBShift+F1\fR opens the Help panel.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B SRS_OPEN
panels to open at start, as for \fB\-\-gui\-open\fR.
.TP
.B SRS_DOC_DIR
directory holding the user guide (default /usr/share/doc/srs-sysinfo).
.TP
.BR SRS_POWER_DB ", " SRS_POWER_INI
use another Power & cost history database or settings file (for tests).
.TP
.B SRS_DATA_DIR
directory holding power-zip-rates.tsv (default: next to the program, then /usr/share/srs-sysinfo).
.B SRS_STARTUP_DRY_RUN
when set to 1, the Startup manager shows the systemctl / pkexec command it would run instead of
running it (autostart files are still written).
.TP
.B SRS_RSMU_DIR
read the ryzen_smu files from this directory instead of /sys/kernel/ryzen_smu_drv (tests).
.SH FILES
.TP
.I /usr/share/doc/srs-sysinfo/SRS-User-Guide.pdf, /usr/share/doc/srs-sysinfo/html/SRS-User-Guide.html
the user guide (PDF and HTML), with its Markdown sources in the same folder.
.TP
.I ~/.config/srs/SRSDEF.ini
settings (theme, update interval, tray, "Classic View=1" for Start in classic view, ...).
.TP
.I ~/.cache/srs/root/
root-only data collected by srs-root.
.TP
.I ~/.config/srs/lineups/*.srslineup
your own tab lineups (the same format as Export lineup).
.TP
.I ~/.config/srs/startup-backup/
autostart files the Startup manager moved aside or changed (nothing is deleted).
.TP
.I ~/.cache/srs/empty-items.txt
items that had nothing to show on this PC at the last check (made only by Options > Check now or
Check daily, which is off by default; the network items and Ping are never checked).
.TP
.I ~/.cache/srs/burnin\-YYYYMMDD\-HHMMSS.txt
the summary of each finished CPU Burnin / CPU Stress run.
.TP
.I ~/.cache/srs/bench\-history.tsv
one line per SRS Pi, Crypto and Prime Sieve run (time, test, settings, result, build).
.I ~/.local/share/srs/power.db
Power & cost history (SQLite, one row per minute, mode 0600), with power.db.lock beside it.
.TP
.I ~/.config/srs/power.ini
Power & cost settings: parts, power supply, price, recording, alarms.
.TP
.I /usr/share/srs-sysinfo/power-zip-rates.tsv
US ZIP code to electric utility table (NREL, CC BY 4.0) for the offline price lookup.
.TP
.I /usr/share/hwdata/pci.ids, /usr/share/hwdata/usb.ids
device name databases.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR sysreport-search (1),
.BR srs-root (1),
.BR srs-sysreport (1)
.SH HOMEPAGE
https://github.com/eugenesflanagin-spec/srs-sysinfo
